\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li1080\tx1080 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls20\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls20\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 Changing the password from a Web Browser.
\par }\pard \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 There are a few precautions that you can take to minimize these risks:
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls21\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls21\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\b\insrsid7218531 Use APOP, AUTH and AUTHORIZE to logon whenever possible}{\insrsid7218531 .  These commands provide a method by which a POP3 or IMAP4 client can log into the server with an encrypted password.  If you POP3 or IMAP4 client supports this capability, then we recommend that you use it.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li1080\tx1080 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls22\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls22\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\b\insrsid7218531 Change Passwords regularly}{\insrsid7218531 .  It is good practice to establish a procedure that requires that your users change their passwords on a regular basis.  This minimizes the exposure to sniffed passwords.  You can use the }{\insrsid15275290 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  Password Server to do this if your mail client supports this capability.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li1080\tx1080 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls23\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls23\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\b\insrsid7218531 Do not create Mailboxes for NT Administrators}{\insrsid7218531 .  Your exposure is magnified if you create a }{\insrsid15275290 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  mailbox based on an NT User with Administrative privileges.  If the password for this user is sniffed then your whole NT Server is compromised.  You can use }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  Aliases and Auto Forwarding capabilities to forward incoming mail for administrative users to a secure Registry Mailbox.  The drawback of this configuration is that the administrative users will have to maintain two passwords.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li1080\tx1080 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls24\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls24\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\b\insrsid7218531 Track Login Failures with the Performance Monitor}{\insrsid7218531 .  You can use the Windows NT Performance Monitor to keep track of failed login attempts.  This is useful to monitor password hacking attempts on the POP3 and IMAP4 servers.  See the }{\uldb\insrsid7218531 Performance Monitor}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 Monitoring}{\insrsid7218531  section for more information.
{\v0\par\v}}\pard\plain \s2\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f1\fs28\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  SMTPSecurity}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  SMTP Security}}}{\insrsid7218531  }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  Security
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  includes numerous security features that allow you control the flow of mail to your site. These include features to block junk email (a.k.a., "spam"), combat third-party relay, and verify the identity of end users who send mail through your site. Setting proper security policies is one of the most important tasks for email administrators, and is crucial for those whose mail server systems are accessible from the Internet.
\par }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  }{\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  SMTP Security}}}{\insrsid7218531 security settings are invoked by double clicking on the }{\uldb\insrsid7218531 Security Properties}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 SMTPSecurity}{\insrsid7218531  icon in the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  }{\insrsid14683656 Management Center}{\insrsid7218531 . You can also use an }{\uldb\insrsid7218531 Anti-Relay Wizard}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 AntiRelayWizard}{\insrsid7218531  and }{\uldb\insrsid7218531 Anti-Spam Wizard}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 AntiSpamWizard}{\insrsid7218531  to assist you in setting these policies.
{\v0\par\v}}{\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid12480280 \{bmc security properties - general 256c.bmp\}}{\insrsid12480280  }{\insrsid7218531\charrsid12480280 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  BasicSecuritySettings}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Basic Security Settings}}}{\insrsid7218531  General Security Settings
\par }\pard\plain \s35\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Included among the options in the General Security Properties window are the following general security preferences:
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  EnablefullVRFYsupport}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Enable full VRFY support}}}{\insrsid7218531  Enable full VRFY support }{\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  VRFY}}}{\insrsid7218531 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 By default, an SMTP command of the form }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 VRFY name@domain.com }{\insrsid7218531 will return a "success" response if the server will accept mail for }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 domain.com}{\insrsid7218531 , regardless of whether the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 name}{\insrsid7218531  is valid.  If you check the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Enable full VRFY support}{\insrsid7218531  box, then the command will only succeed if }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 name@domain.com }{\insrsid7218531 is a valid mailbox or a mail list (or is an alias). If you leave this box unchecked, it reduces the amount of information an attacker or spammer can find out about your users. For that reason, it is recommended that you do not enable this option.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  AuthenticatedETRN}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Authenticated ETRN}}}{\insrsid7218531  Authenticated ETRN }{\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  ETRN}}}{\insrsid7218531 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 The SMTP }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 ETRN}{\insrsid7218531  command is used to request an SMTP server to start sending mail for a particular domain.  (This command is used by the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 MSSTART}{\insrsid7218531  utility.)  If you check the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Authenticated ETRN}{\insrsid7218531  option, then the SMTP client must be authenticated (using the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 AUTH}{\insrsid7218531  command with a valid mailbox name and password), otherwise the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 ETRN}{\insrsid7218531  command will be rejected.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  MaximumMessageSize}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Maximum message size}}}{\insrsid7218531  Maximum Message Size }{\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Maximum message size}}}{\insrsid7218531 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 The maximum message size in bytes that the SMTP Receiver will accept. The default is 0, which means that there is no size limit (apart from memory and disk space constraints). To prevent excessively large messages from impacting your system, it is recommended that you set this value to 10 MB (10,000,000 bytes) or less.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  AntiDenialofServicePolicies}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Anti-Denial of Service Policies}}}{\insrsid7218531  Anti-Denial of Service Policies
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 In addition to spam and relaying, }{\i\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 denial-of-service}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  (DoS) attacks are a significant threat to the smooth operation of a mail server. DoS attacks involve one or more systems bombarding your SMTP server with millions of connections and/or messages. These attacks can cause you mail server system to perform very poorly as it tries to cope with the exceptionally high loads.
\par }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  includes policies that allow you to contain DoS attacks to prevent them from overwhelming your server system. The anti-DoS features provided in the }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  Security window are the following:
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Max connections (all hosts)
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 This setting provides a limit to the total number of incoming SMTP connections that }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  will respond to at any time. When this number of connections are active, SMTPRA will no longer accept new connections until a current connection has been closed.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Max connections per host
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 This setting limits the number of incoming SMTP connections allowed from any one system. When this number of SMTP connections from any one host are active, SMTPRA will no longer accept new connections from that host. This option is very useful for containing potential damage from an abusive system without blocking incoming connections from other (non-abusive) systems.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Connection timeout
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 This option sets a limit to the number of seconds that }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  will keep open a non-responsive SMTP connection.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Limit throughput from each host
\par }\pard\plain \s35\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 This option provides a throttle on the amount of data (in kilobytes per second) that SMTPRA will accept from any one host. This allows you to control DoS attacks that send large amounts of data over relatively few connections. When using this option it is recommended that you exempt your "friendly" hosts from this limit to avoid slowing mail traffic from your own network.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  AntiSpamPolicies}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Anti-Spam Policies}}}{\insrsid7218531  Anti-Spam Policies
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\lang1024\langfe1024\super\noproof\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Spam}}}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 's anti-spam policies allow you to block mail from specific hosts or users, and include the following options:
\par }\pard \ql \tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 {\pard\plain \ql \tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\cgrid\noproof\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid12480280 \{bmc security properties - anti-spam 256c.bmp\}}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid12480280  }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531\charrsid12480280 
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-720\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls143\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 {\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Block mail from unknown hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . Most legitimate sources of email are hosts that can be located in the DNS. In other words, these machines are well-established and correspond to known }{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 host.domain}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  names. Many systems used to distrubute spam are not defined in the DNS, and are accessible only through TCP/IP address (i.e., }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 12.34.56.78}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 ). Be aware that blocking mail from unknown hosts is a very conservative policy and may lead to legitimate mail being blocked from your users.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Block mail from blacklisted hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . Sources of high-volume spam are tracked by }{\cs98\lang1024\langfe1024\super\noproof\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Blacklist}}}{\i\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 blacklist services}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 , which provide lists of sites that have been responsible for such mailings. }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  allows you to block all messages from hosts named by a blacklist service, and also allows you to select the blacklist service you wish to use. The default service is the MAPS Realtime Blackhole List (RBL). Use of this feature is recommended for ISP sites.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Block mail from specific hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . In addition to unknown hosts and blacklisted hosts, you can block mail from any other source. You can choose to block mail from servers based on hostname or IP address. As with other blocking methods, this prevents the named system from sending any mail - spam or otherwise - to your site, so it should be used conservatively.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Block mail from specific users}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . Mail can also be blocked from specific users, as defined by the envelope return address (}{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 MAIL FROM}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 ). This option is particularly useful if one or more specific users are sending junk mail to your users. When blocking addresses, you can use wildcard notation (}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 *@domain.com}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 ) to block mail coming from all addresses within a domain.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Set maximum recipients per message}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . Because spam is typically sent to large numbers of users, setting a maximum on the number of recipients per message allows you to combat mass mailings. When you set a limit to the number of recipients, messages sent to more than this number of addresses (as given by the }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 RCPT TO}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  command) will have all recipients after the maximum number rejected. For example, if you set a maximum of 20, and a message arrives for 50 of your users, only the first 20 users will receive the message.
\par }\pard \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 All of these policies can be set in the Anti-Spam tab of the Security Properties window. }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  also provides an Anti-Spam wizard that leads you through the process of implementing an anti-spam strategy for your site. Using the wizard is highly recommened, particularly for new users. For more information, double-click the }{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Anti-Spam Wizard}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  icon in the }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid14683656 Management Center}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 's Server folder.
\par Experienced users can also use mask lists to specify hosts and addresses which should be blocked from sending mail to your site. These lists can be opened from the Anti-Spam Tab of the Security Properties window by clicking the buttons }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Reject all incoming mail from these addresses}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 , and }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Reject all incoming mail from these hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . See the following section on }{\uldb\insrsid7218531 Advanced SMTP Security}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 AdvancedSMTPSecurity}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  for more information.
{\v0\par\v}}\pard\plain \s30\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\uldb\insrsid7218531 Mail Filters}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 MailFilters}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  (described in the following chapter) provide an additional mechanism to combat spam. Filters allow you to search the contents of the message (headers, body, and attachments) for specific  content and block it from entering your system. For example, using filters you can reject all mail that contains the subject "Make money fast!", regardless of the host used to send the mail.
{\v0\par\v}}\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  AntiRelayPolicies}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Anti-Relay Policies}}}{\insrsid7218531  Anti-Relay Policies
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\lang1024\langfe1024\super\noproof\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Relay}}}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Along with spam, mail administrators must contend with a type of abuse known as third-party relay. Relay occurs when your mail server accepts a message addressed to another site and sends it to the proper destination. Third-party relay is a scenario in which a distributor of junk mail uses another site's mail server to distribute his/her mass mailing. This can result in a loss of your network resources and can even cause your site to be blacklisted as a source of spam.
\par To combat third-party relay }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  includes policies to restrict relaying, including:
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls143\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 {\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Require SMTP authentication for relay}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . The simplest technique for combating third-party relay, this option requires that users provide a login name and password that matches a mailbox on your site before they are allowed to relay mail to other sites. You can also specify that users connecting from "friendly" sites (for example, your own network) are exempt from this requirement.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Allow relay only from specific hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . This option specifies the hosts from which relay is allowed. Typically, this is a range of IP addresses that correspond to your own network. When relay is allowed only from your own network, third-party relay from outside your network is prevented. 
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Disable relay for non-local users}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . This option disallows relay if the return address of a message is not within one of your domains. 
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Require valid return address for relay}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . This option disallows relay if the return address of a message contains an unknown domain (or a domain for which no mail records exist in the DNS).
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Allow relay to specific domains}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . This option allows }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  to accept relay for specific domains, such as friendly sites. This allows you to set strict relay-prevention policies while still allowing delivery to domains for which your site is a gateway.
\par }\pard \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 As with anti-spam features, all of these relay policies can be set in the Anti-Spam tab of the Security Properties window or with a wizard. Because of the complexities of relay, it is highly recommended that administrators use the Anti-Relay Wizard to set these policies. For more information, double-click the }{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Anti-Spam Wizard}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  icon in the }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid14683656 Management Center}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 's Server folder.
\par Experienced users can also use mask lists to specify hosts and addresses for which relay is allowed or disallowed. These lists can opened from the Security window by clicking the buttons }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Accept mail for relay to these doimains}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 , and }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 Accept mail for relay from these friendly hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 . See the following section on }{\uldb\insrsid7218531 Advanced SMTP Security}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 AdvancedSMTPSecurity}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531  for more information.
{\v0\par\v}}\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  SMTPAuthentication}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  SMTP Authentication}}}{\insrsid7218531  SMTP Authentication
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\lang1024\langfe1024\super\noproof\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  SMTP authentication}}}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 As described above, SMTP autentication is one of the best ways to combat third-party relay because it requires that relay is permitted only for authorized users of your site. Because it requires a login name and password, SMTP authentication makes relay as secure as POP3 and IMAP4 mailbox access. However, this option requires that your users have mail clients that support SMTP authentication, which is not included with all clients.
\par When using SMTP authentication, you can also specify that users are exempt from this requirement if they are within your own network (or from other specific hosts). This allows you to support your dial-up users whose mail clients do not support SMTP authentication.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\i0\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  DHAPSettings}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Directory Harvest Attack Prevention (DHAP) Settings}}}{\i0\insrsid7218531  }{\insrsid7218531\charrsid13316901 Directory Harvest Attack Protection (DHAP) Settings}{\i0\insrsid7218531 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  provides a mechanism for identifying a directory/dictionary harvest attack or }{\b\insrsid7218531 DHA}{\insrsid7218531  (otherwise known as email harvesting) and preventing the attack from continuing for a "block duration" configurable in the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  }{\insrsid14683656 Management Center}{\insrsid7218531 . When the block duration expires the host regains access but may be blocked again. 
\par When a host is deemed a harvester its IP address, host name, the time of blocking, block duration and the total number of times blocked is written to the SMTPRA log and the host is disconnected.
\par You may wish to permanently block an IP address that has been identified as a harvesting address using the "Reject all incoming mail from these hosts option" in the SMTP security dialogue or by using your firewall.
\par }\pard \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\sa120\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 DHAP settings are invoked by clicking on the DHAP Settings tab in the Security Properties dialog in the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  admin }{\insrsid14683656 Management Center}{\insrsid7218531 .
\par For more information on how to configure }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  DHAP settings, please refer to the }{\b\insrsid7218531 Windows Administration }{\b\insrsid14683656 Management Center}{\insrsid7218531  section of this manual.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f1\fs28\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  AdvancedSMTPSecurity}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Advanced SMTP Security}}}{\insrsid7218531  Advanced SMTP Security
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof\insrsid7218531 When setting up SMTP security features you can use the Anti-Spam and Anti-Relay wizards to easily set these policies. If you want greater control over SMTP security, you can also modify certain settings directly through the Security window. This section provides information on manually setting spam and relay policies through the use of mask lists.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  Usingthemasklists}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Using the mask lists}}}{\insrsid7218531  Using the mask lists
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 If a call comes in to }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531 's SMTP server from a host whose name, IP address, or }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 EHLO}{\insrsid7218531  identification matches the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Reject all incoming mail from these hosts}{\insrsid7218531  list, then all attempts by that host to transfer mail to }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  will be rejected when the client issues the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 MAIL FROM}{\insrsid7218531  command. (Note that you should check the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Lookup SMTP hosts in the DNS}{\insrsid7218531  box to get maximum benefit from this feature.)
\par If a connection comes in to }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  from a host that does not appear in the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Accept mail for relay from these friendly hosts}{\insrsid7218531  list, then }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  will refuse to accept mail from that host for recipients other than local mailboxes, unless the destination mail domain appears in the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Accept mail for relay to these domains}{\insrsid7218531  list.
\par If a }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 MAIL FROM:}{\insrsid7218531  command is issued with an e-mail address that appears in the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Reject all incoming mail from these addresses}{\insrsid7218531  list, the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 MAIL FROM}{\insrsid7218531  command will be rejected.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  SpamPolicies}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Spam Policies}}}{\insrsid7218531  Spam Policies
\par }\pard\plain \s35\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 When SMTP mask lists are enabled, }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  uses these masks to define a }{\i\insrsid7218531 spam policy}{\insrsid7218531  for each SMTP connection.  These policies define which (if any) messages will be accepted during the SMTP session.  The spam policies used by }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  are:
\par {\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s35\ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls138\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls138\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\b\insrsid7218531 AcceptAllMail}{\insrsid7218531 , which causes all messages to be accepted regardless of sender and recipient.
\par {\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s35\ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls138\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls138\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\b\insrsid7218531 AcceptLocalMailOnly}{\insrsid7218531 , which causes messages to be accepted only if they are addressed to mailboxes or mail lists within your }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  domains.
\par {\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s35\ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls138\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls138\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\b\insrsid7218531 RejectAllMail}{\insrsid7218531 , which causes all messages to be rejected regardless of sender and recipient.
\par {\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\s35 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s35\ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls138\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls138\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\b\insrsid7218531 RejectAllMailFrom}{\insrsid7218531 , which causes messages to be rejected only if they are from one or more specific users.
