lbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth2880\clshdrawnil \cellx3240\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth5508\clshdrawnil \cellx8748\row }\pard \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 This setting rejects mail that is addressed to this mail list and from }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 spammer@spamdomain.com}{\insrsid7218531 .  Likewise mail from any user in }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 evildomain}{\insrsid7218531  (except mail from }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 knownperson@evildomain}{\insrsid7218531 ) would be blocked.  All other messages to the mailing list would also be processed as usual.
\par }\pard \ql \tx0\li0\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn0\lin0 {\insrsid7218531 Don't Accept Commands From:\cell }{\field\fldedit{\*\fldinst {\b\f2\uldb\insrsid7218531  HYPERLINK mailto:me@mydomain }{\b\f2\uldb\insrsid5271419 {\*\datafield 78}}}{\fldrslt {\cs33\uldb\insrsid7218531 me@mydomain}}}{\b\v\f2\uldb\insrsid7218531 !EF("mailto:me@mydomain")}{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 \txne\line *\cell }\pard \ql \tx0\li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\txn0\lin0 {\insrsid7218531 \trowd \irow0\irowband0\lastrow \ts11\trgaph108\trleft360\trbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrh\brdrs\brdrw10 \trbrdrv\brdrs\brdrw10 \trftsWidth1\trpaddl108\trpaddr108\trpaddfl3\trpaddfr3 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth2880\clshdrawnil \cellx3240\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrl\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrb\brdrs\brdrw10 \clbrdrr\brdrs\brdrw10 \cltxlrtb\clftsWidth3\clwWidth5508\clshdrawnil \cellx8748\row }\pard \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 This setting rejects all mail to the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 list-request}{\insrsid7218531  address unless it is from }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 me@mydomain}{\insrsid7218531 .
\par It is relatively easy for knowledgeable people to forge e-mail addresses so that messages appear to come from someone else. This feature will not block these messages.
\par Refer to the }{\uldb\insrsid7218531 List Security Page}{\v\uldb\insrsid7218531 ListSecurityPage}{\insrsid7218531  in the }{\insrsid14683656 Management Center}{\insrsid7218531  section for more information.
{\v0\par\v}}\pard\plain \s2\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f1\fs28\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  TransportLayerSecurity}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Transport Layer Security (TLS/SSL)}}}{\insrsid7218531  Transport Layer Security (TLS/SSL)
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Security is a critical issue for system administrators. The best system administrators work to protect their email systems and are on constant guard against unauthorized system access.
\par A protocol is a standard format that enables two devices to transmit and receive date. In the world of Internet communications, protocols are the agreed-upon rules for messaging components, such as handshaking, message delimitation, and transmission mode and rate.
\par Email communications, Web server and browser interactions (such as online commerce), and telecommuting would not be possible}{\insrsid7218531 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 190 \\f "Symbol" \\s 8}{\fldrslt\f3\fs16\'be}}}{\insrsid7218531 or safe}{\insrsid7218531 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 190 \\f "Symbol" \\s 8}{\fldrslt\f3\fs16\'be}}}{\insrsid7218531 without these accepted protocols.
\par In this software release, }{\insrsid1670342 Eudora}{\insrsid7218531  has increased the security of }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  with the inclusion of the Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) protocol. Secure Sockets Layer protocol, initially developed by Netscape Corporation, is a security protocol that provides communications privacy over the Internet. The protocol allows client/server applications to communicate in a way that is designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering, or message forgery.
\par Transport Layer Security (TLS), more commonly known as SSL, is a popular mechanism for enhancing TCP communications with privacy and authentication. TLS or Transport Layer Security is the IETF-endorsed version of SSL. TLS is a standard closely related to SSL 3.0, and is sometimes referred to as "SSL 3.1". }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  now supports both TLS 1.0 ("SSL 3.1") and SSL 3.0.
\par The SSL/TLS protocol uses signed digital certificates and a special cryptography method, called public key, for authentication.  This method of authentication furnishes a way to verify the identity of each communicating pair, providing improved security not only for a particular email exchange, but also for the mail server and the system that host the exchange. In short, the SSL/TLS protocol enables the secure encryption of messages transmitted between client and server and between server and server. Administrators should be aware, however, that this added encryption and decryption activity places an extra processing load on the server.
\par While a full examination of digital certificates and public key cryptography is beyond the scope of this manual, brief descriptions of both are provided to assist system administrators in their duties.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Certificates 
\par }\pard\plain \s35\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Digital certificates provide:
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-720\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls136\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls136\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Authentication}{\insrsid7218531 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 190 \\f "Symbol" \\s 8}{\fldrslt\f3\fs16\'be}}}{\insrsid7218531 information stored on a certificate verifies that the person sending a message is who he or she asserts to be. 
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls136\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls136\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 Encryption}{\insrsid7218531 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 190 \\f "Symbol" \\s 8}{\fldrslt\f3\fs16\'be}}}{\insrsid7218531 the encryption safeguards email so that it only those who are authorized can read it. 
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls136\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls136\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 Security}{\insrsid7218531 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 190 \\f "Symbol" \\s 8}{\fldrslt\f3\fs16\'be}}}{\insrsid7218531 certificates help safeguard private information that is shared during exchanges, such as credit card information shared during online commerce transactions.
\par }\pard \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 A certificate provides this protection by means of the information that is stored on it and the cryptographic keys that are linked with this stored data. The distinguished name (DN) format (the X.509 standard) defines how and what information is stored on certificates. Much like a paper-based driver's or business license that is kept in a wallet or in an office file and proffered to prove identity, a digital certificate contains identification information that is stored on a machine's hard disk. This identification information includes the common name (CO), organization name, city, state, and country location associated with a particular computer. Each certificate includes a serial number and the time period for which the certificate is valid (different certificates expire at different times). The private key and public key pair provides the encryption used with the certificate (see "Public Key Cryptography" below). 
\par A company or entity that generates certificates is known as a certificate authority (CA). Many companies, including VeriSign/Thawte, GeoTrust, and Comodo (InstantSSL), sell digital certificates to individuals and businesses. An alternative to third-party certificates are self-signed certificates. Usually these certificates are created by an administrator that acts as his or her own certificate authority. (See the "Certificate Requirements" section of this chapter for more information about creating and installing certificates).
\par Although }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  will work with self-signed certificates, we recommend that you obtain a certificate from a known and trusted certificate authority (CA). 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Public Key Cryptography 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Public key cryptography was developed over a quarter of a century ago. It is sometimes referred to as asymmetric encryption because it uses two keys}{\insrsid7218531 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 190 \\f "Symbol" \\s 8}{\fldrslt\f3\fs16\'be}}}{\insrsid7218531 a public key and a private key. This cryptography system provides the authentication safeguards for digital certificates. Private key security is safeguarded by the numeric formulas and the number of digits (more than 100) used in these cryptographic operations.
\par As its name implies, the public key is known to everyone and is used to encrypt an email message. The private key is known to only one entity (the message recipient) and is used to decrypt the message. Here is how it works: Jack sends an encrypted message to Jill by using Jill's public key. Once she receives this message, Jill uses her private key to decrypt the email. 
\par Of course, Jack and Jill don't really have to worry about such encryption and decryption chores}{\insrsid7218531 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 190 \\f "Symbol" \\s 8}{\fldrslt\f3\fs16\'be}}}{\insrsid7218531 their mail software and certificates manage this activity in the background. 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Configuring }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  to Use SSL/TLS
\par }\pard\plain \s35\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 The system administrator must perform the following steps to configure }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  to enable the new SSL/TLS protocol feature:
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls155\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls155\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Obtain a certificate from a trusted certificate authority or create a self-signed certificate (see the "Generating a Test Certificate" section that follows). A system administrator may want to use a self-signed certificate for testing purposes.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls155\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls155\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\insrsid7218531 Install the certificate in the Personal folder of the Computer certificate store using the MMC Certificates snap-in.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls155\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls155\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\insrsid7218531 Use the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  Security Properties window to configure the appropriate level of SSL/TLS deployment. The three possible levels are: Disable TLS, Allow TLS, and Require TLS.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls155\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls155\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\insrsid7218531 Use the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  Security Properties window to "Enable alternate SSL port" usage for POP and IMAP. Check the "Enable" checkbox in both the POP3 and IMAP4 areas of the Security Properties window and specify the port to be used in each of the adjacent text boxes.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\jclisttab\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls155\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'b7}}\tx720 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls155\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0\pararsid15231392 {\insrsid7218531 Start or restart the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  services.
\par }\pard \ql \tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 {\pard\plain \ql \tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid9723326 \{bmc security properties - tls - 256c.bmp\}}{\insrsid9723326  }{\insrsid7218531\charrsid9723326 
\par }\pard\plain \s94\ql \tx360\li360\ri130\sb120\sa120\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn360\lin360\itap0 \b\f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Certificate Requirements
\par }\pard\plain \s35\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\sa120\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 The certificate must meet several requirements before the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  server is able to use it to secure email connections.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls159\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 The }{\i\insrsid7218531 Intended Purposes}{\insrsid7218531  of the certificate must include Server Authentication. Certificates purchased from trusted certificate authorities or issued by a domain certificate authority using the Web Server template will include this intended purpose.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\tx720\li720\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls163\adjustright\rin130\txn720\lin720\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 The }{\i\insrsid7218531 Common Name (CN) }{\insrsid7218531 of the certificate must match the hostname of the Reverse DNS (PTR) record for the IP addresses that the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  services are listening on or the Windows HOSTS file must be updated to override the PTR record returned by your DNS servers. }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  does a reverse DNS lookup to find out the Common Name of the certificate to use before searching the certificate store for an appropriate certificate.\txne\line \txne\line Example: The network adapters in your mail server are assigned the IP addresses 192.168.1.10 and 192.168.1.11 and your DNS servers report that the reverse DNS entries for 192.168.1.10 and 192.168.1.11 are both mail.yourcorp.com. }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  will look for a certificate with a common name of mail.yourcorp.com in the certificate store.\txne\line \txne\line If }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  is unable to locate an appropriate certificate it will log the following error indicating the name of the Common Name of the certificate that it attempted to locate:\txne\line \txne\line }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 TLS/SSL: The service failed to find a suitable certificate in the predefined MY System store for the LocalMachine\txne\line      : No certificates were found matching the Subject 'smtp.yourcorp.com'.}{\insrsid7218531 \txne\line \txne\line The HOSTS files located in the %SYSTEMROOT%\\SYSTEM32\\DRIVERS\\ETC on your server can be updated to override the reverse DNS entries and point }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  to the appropriate certificate.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}The certificate must be stored in the Personal folder of the Computer certificate store.  This is the same location where web server certificates are stored.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}The }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  services must have permission to read and use the private key of the certificate in the certificate store.  If the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  services are running in a secured mode as a user account without administrator privileges then the system administrator must explicitly grant the necessary permissions.\txne\line \txne\line The WinHTTP Certificate Configuration Tool (WinHTTPCertCfg) is a command-line tool that enables administrators to import certificates and set permissions.  It is located in the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  installation directory.\txne\line \txne\line }{\b\insrsid7218531 Example: Listing accounts with access to the private key\txne\line }{\insrsid7218531 \txne\line The task in this example is to determine which Windows accounts have permission to access the certificate's private key.  To do this use the }{\f2\insrsid7218531 /l}{\insrsid7218531  to request a list of accounts, }{\f2\insrsid7218531 /c}{\insrsid7218531  }{\f2\insrsid7218531 local_machine\\my}{\insrsid7218531  to select the Personal folder of the Computer certificate store and }{\f2\insrsid7218531 /s mail.mycorp.com}{\insrsid7218531  for the certificate with a Common Name of }{\f2\insrsid7218531 mail.mycorp.com}{\insrsid7218531 . \txne\line \txne\line }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 winhttpcertcfg /l /c local_machine\\my /s mail.mycorp.com}{\insrsid7218531 \txne\line \txne\line }{\b\insrsid7218531 Example: Granting Access to a Private Key from a User Account\txne\line }{\insrsid7218531 \txne\line The task in this example is to grant access to a certificate's private key to a user account. To do this, use }{\f2\insrsid7218531 /g}{\insrsid7218531  to grant access, }{\f2\insrsid7218531 /c local_machine\\my}{\insrsid7218531  to select the Personal folder of the Computer certificate store, }{\f2\insrsid7218531 /s mail.mycorp.com}{\insrsid7218531  to select the certificate with the common name }{\f2\insrsid7218531 mail.mycorp.com}{\insrsid7218531  and }{\f2\insrsid7218531 /a MYCORP\\}{\f2\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\f2\insrsid7218531 -User}{\insrsid7218531  to grant access to the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531 -User account in the MYCORP domain.\txne\line \txne\line }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 winhttpcertcfg /g /c local_machine\\my /s mail.mycorp.com /a MYCORP\\}{\b\f2\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 -User}{\insrsid7218531 
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\fi-360\li720\tx720 \tab}{\*\listtextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}Certificates use a variety of cryptographic algorithms.  }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  requires a certificate that specifies the }{\f2\insrsid7218531 Microsoft RSA SChannel Cryptographic Provider}{\insrsid7218531  and the }{\f2\insrsid7218531 SHA-1}{\insrsid7218531  hash algorithm.  The cryptographic provider and hash algorithm are specified when requesting your certificate from the certificate authority.\txne\line 
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa40\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Generating a Test Certificate
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 For testing purposes, create a self-signed X.509 certificate using the }{\f2\insrsid7218531 makecert}{\insrsid7218531  utility provided with the Core SDK component of the Windows Platform SDK.  The certificate must identify the common name of the }{\insrsid536461 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  server as specified above in the Certificate Requirements and the extended key usage as "server authentication" (OID 1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.1).  For example:
\par }{\f2\insrsid7218531 makecert -r -pe -n "CN=mail.mycorp.com" -b 01/01/2001 -e 01/01/2099 -eku 1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.1 -ss my -sr localMachine -a sha1 -sky exchange -sp "Microsoft RSA SChannel Cryptographic Provider" -sy 12
\par }{\insrsid7218531 Open the Microsoft management console and add the local computer certificate snap-in.  Check that the certificate has been created in the personal store and copy it to the trusted root certification authority.
\par 
