\par }{\insrsid7218531 Normally, }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  uses DNS MX records to decide which host to contact in order to deliver mail for a particular domain.  In some circumstances it may be necessary to augment or replace the use of the DNS records by manual routing information.  For example, you may wish to use }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  on a proxy relay server to route incoming messages to a secure mail server on the LAN.  This form allows you to set up a manual routing table to do this.
\par The manual routing table consists of an ordered list of entries that are shown in a scrolling list.  Each entry consists of a domain mask, a combination rule, and one or more associated routes.
\par The order of the entries in the manual routing table is significant.  It is processed top-to-bottom, and the first matching entry is used.  Thus, more specific entries (not containing wildcards) should be positioned near the top of the list, and less specific entries (containing wildcards) towards the end of the list. Use the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Up}{\insrsid7218531  and }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Down}{\insrsid7218531  buttons to move entries around.
\par To specify the routes on which messages for the domain are sent, select a domain mask and click the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Routes}{\insrsid7218531  button.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Domain Mask
