\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid7218531 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  DNS}}}{\insrsid7218531 The Internet is a global network of computers connected using the TCP/IP protocol.  Each computer has a unique numerical TCP/IP address that other computers use to communicate with it.
\par People find it easier to refer to computers by name.  Domain Name Service (DNS) takes care of this dilemma.  It's sole purpose in life is to translate (or }{\i\insrsid7218531 resolve}{\insrsid7218531 , in technical terminology) TCP/IP addresses to computer names (and vice-versa).
\par DNS services run on many computers on the Internet.  DNS is designed so that if a computer is asked to resolve an address and does not know the answer, it will pass the request on to a higher level authority.
\par Your computer, as a DNS client, will submit resolution requests to DNS servers when you try to connect to Internet sites such as }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 www.ibm.com}{\insrsid7218531 .  The primary and secondary DNS servers for your computer are entered in numerical format in the Network TCP/IP Settings.
\par The DNS database on the server contains a number of different mappings of names to addresses, called record types.  The most common, A, is the one that most people think of as DNS. A records are the ones used for resolving WWW and FTP addresses. In general you will find one numeric address for a given host name. For example, the A record for }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 www.ibm.com}{\insrsid7218531  is:
\par {\pard\plain\s32 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\s32 \f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard\plain \s32\ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls88\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls88\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 165.87.194.133
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 There is a second record type is called Mail eXchange (MX) records.  These records are devoted to the delivery of mail. A MX record for a particular name will have several addresses and priorities. For example, to send e-mail to DEC, you need to use the name }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 dec.com}{\insrsid7218531 . DEC has the following MX records, each with a different priority number:
\par }\pard\plain \s35\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp80 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp20 \brdrr\brdrs\brdrw10\brsp80 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\rtlgutter\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f2\insrsid7218531     16.1.0.22          inet-gw-2.pa.dec.com   70\txne\line     16.1.0.33          inet-gw-3.pa.dec.com   90\txne\line     16.1.0.23          inet-gw-2.pa.dec.com   100
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 These records tell mail software where to send the mail and what to do if the first machine is not available. If the mail server }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 inet-gw-2.pa.dec.com}{\insrsid7218531  is being rebooted, a mail server will send mail to }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 inet-gw-3.pa.dec.com}{\insrsid7218531  (since these are the entries with the next higher priority in the MX record).
\par Many destinations do not have MX records. In this case, mail software will attempt to use the A instead.  Mail will be held until it is possible forward if none of these machines are available.
\par The following points should be noted:
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li115\tx115 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls80\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 It is wise to ask your provider to act as a secondary mail server for you in case your machine becomes unavailable.  For example, your disks could become full, your Internet connection could break.
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li1080\tx1080 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls80\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 The MX records and A records for the same name could be different (thus one machine receives mail and another machine receives all other traffic).
\par {\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\fi-720\li1080\tx1080 \tab}{\*\pntextbsx\pard\plain\f3\fs16\insrsid7218531 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-720\tx1080\li1080\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\jclisttab{\*\pnbsx \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang {\pntxtb \'de}}\tx1080 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls80\adjustright\rin130\txn1080\lin1080\itap0 {\insrsid7218531 If MX records exist, they }{\i\insrsid7218531 must}{\insrsid7218531  be correct, otherwise your domain will not receive mail.
