\par }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 -version
\par }{\insrsid7218531 Display the version number of the mail server.
\par }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 -machine \\\\name
\par }{\insrsid7218531 Specify the name of the target }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  server. If this is omitted, the local machine is assumed. Note that the machine name must be preceded by a double backslash.
\par }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 -restore
\par }{\insrsid7218531 Read the configuration from the file and write it to the Registry. If this argument is omitted, the configuration will be read from the Registry and written to the file.
\par }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 -deleteold
\par }{\insrsid7218531 Delete the old configuration in the Registry before restoring the configuration from the file. If this argument is omitted, the configuration from the file will be merged with the existing configuration. This argument is only relevant if }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 -restore}{\insrsid7218531  is specified. 
\par }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 filename
\par }{\insrsid7218531 The name of the file to which the configuration will be written (or from which it will be read, if -restore was specified). This argument is required.
\par }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 MSBACK}{\insrsid7218531  uses a text format to store the mail server configuration information. The format is described in }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 BNF}{\insrsid7218531  below.  Examining a generated backup file will help make this clearer.
\par }\pard\plain \s52\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp20 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp80 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp20 \brdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp80 \aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\rtlgutter\itap0 \b\f2\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 <configurationfile> ::= <key> [<key>]\txne\line <key> ::= <openingmarker>[<value>..][<subkey>..]<closingmarker>\txne\line <openingmarker> ::= "["<escapedkeyname>"]"\txne\line <closingmarker> ::= "[""/"<escapedkeyname>"]"\txne\line <escapedkeyname> ::= key name, control chars, %, / [ and ] chars replaced by %xx \txne\line <value> ::= <valuename> "=" <valuedata>\txne\line <valuename> ::= value name with any control chars, % and = chars replaced by %xx\txne\line <valuedata> ::= <dwordvaluedata>|<stringvaluedata>|<multistringvaluedata>\txne\line <dwordvaluedata> ::= string of decimal digits\txne\line <stringvaluedata> ::= '"'[<stringdata>]'"'\txne\line <stringdata> ::= sequence of chars with any control chars, % and " replaced by %xx\txne\line <multistringvaluedata> ::= <stringvaluedata>[<stringvaluedata>..]'"''"'\txne\line <subkey> ::= <key>
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\insrsid7218531 Note that an empty multistring is represented as }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 "" ""}{\insrsid7218531  whereas an empty string is represented as }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 ""}{\insrsid7218531 .\~ It is vital that the opening and closing markers and each value are on a line by themselves - i.e. no newlines within }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 <openmarker>}{\insrsid7218531 , }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 <closemarker>}{\insrsid7218531  or }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 <value>}{\insrsid7218531 .
\par You can backup the }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  configuration at any time.\~ However, it is important to stop all services before restoring the configuration.
