\par }\pard\plain \s90\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb120\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0\pararsid8200725 \b\f1\fs40\cf17\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs113\f4\insrsid7218531\charrsid1928375 \page }{\cs98\cf0\super\insrsid7218531 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 #}{\insrsid15606814  Glossary}}${\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 $}{\insrsid15606814  Glossary}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 K}{\insrsid15606814  Glossary}}+{\footnote \pard\plain \s97\ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs98\super\insrsid15606814 +}{\insrsid15606814  mailsite:0000}}}{\cs113\f4\insrsid7218531\charrsid1928375  Appendix M - Glossary
\par }\pard\plain \ql \tx115\li115\ri130\sb80\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin130\txn115\lin115\itap0 \f1\fs16\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\insrsid7218531 Agent: }{\insrsid7218531 An agent is a program that is executed by }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  whenever a message arrives to the system, to a mailbox, to a mail list, or to a mail list processing address. Agents allow you to extend }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531 's functionality by adding sophisticated message-handling abilities, such as checking e-mail for virus attachments or archiving messages.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Alias: }{\insrsid7218531 An alias is a delivery rule that causes messages received for a particular e-mail address to be redirected to another e-mail address. maps one e-mail address to another. mapping of one e-email 
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Auto-reply: }{\insrsid7218531 An auto-reply is a message that is automatically sent in response to mail received by a mailbox. Auto-reply messages are typically used to distribute frequently requested information, or to alert senders that you'll be away from your e-mail for a few days and won't be able to immediately respond.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Cluster: }{\insrsid7218531 A cluster is a group of computers that act as a unified system. }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531 's clustering allows multiple server machines (knows as nodes) to host mailboxes for a single domain.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 DNS: }{\insrsid7218531 Domain Name Service (DNS) is a name lookup service that translates familiar internet names (such as }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 ibm.com}{\insrsid7218531 ) into numeric TCP/IP addresses (such as }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 129.34.139.30}{\insrsid7218531 ) that computers understand.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Digest: }{\insrsid7218531 A digest is a collection of messages sent to a mail list that is distributed as a single e-mail. Digests are convenient because they allow users to receive one large message instead of having their mailbox cluttered with dozens of messages.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 ESMTP: }{\insrsid7218531 ESMTP stands for Extended SMTP.  ESMTP defines a number of extensions to the SMTP protocol as defined in RFC1854, RFC1869 and RFC1870.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Event Log: }{\insrsid7218531 The Windows NT Event Log records operating system errors, events and incidents.  You can use the Event Viewer to review the entries in the event log.  The Event Viewer can open event logs on remote computers.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 HOSTS: }{\insrsid7218531 The HOSTS table is used to maintain a local list of TCP/IP names and addresses.  On your Windows NT machine the HOSTS table can be found in:  }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 %WINDIR%\\SYSTEM32\\DRIVERS\\ETC\\HOSTS}{\insrsid7218531 .  On your Windows 95 machine the HOSTS table can be found in:  }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 %WINDIR%. }{\insrsid7218531  The file is in ASCII format and can be edited using }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 NOTEPAD}{\insrsid7218531 .  Any changes will take effect immediately.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 HTML: }{\insrsid7218531 HTML stands for Hyper Text Markup Language.  HTML is the language of the World Wide Web.  HTML is often used to present entry forms for users to enter and update information from their Web browser.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 HTTP: }{\insrsid7218531 HTTP stands for Hyper Text Transfer Protocol.  This is the communication language of the World Wide Web.  All Web clients and Web servers communicate using this protocol.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 IMAP:}{\insrsid7218531   IMAP stands for Internet Mail Access Protocol.  IMAP is an Internet standard that advances the capability of POP.  IMAP provides all of the functionality found in POP.  In addition it provides access to a comprehensive message and folder store on the server.  }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  has implemented this standard in the IMAP4 Service.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Internet Mail Address: }{\insrsid7218531 The standard format for an Internet Mail Address is }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 user@host.domain}{\insrsid7218531 , where }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 user}{\insrsid7218531  is the user account on the host that the mail message will be delivered to, }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 host}{\insrsid7218531  is the name of the mail host and }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 domain}{\insrsid7218531  is the name of the internet domain.  This address standard is defined in RFC822.  See the DNS Overview for more information on Internet domain naming.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Internet: }{\insrsid7218531 The Internet is the global network of computers that communicate using the standard TCP/IP protocol.  All mail on the Internet is delivered using the standard SMTP protocol.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 LAN: }{\insrsid7218531 Abbreviation for Local Area Network.  This is the computer network internal to your organization.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Loop: }{\insrsid7218531 A mail loop is an error condition that occurs when a message is repeatedly routed through the same mail systems. Loops typically result from errors in DNS setup.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Mailbox: }{\insrsid7218531 An electronic mailbox is an individual e-mail account that typically corresponds to a person who uses it to send and receive mail.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Mail list: }{\insrsid7218531 A mail list is a special e-mail account that forwards all received messages to the members of the list.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 MIME: }{\insrsid7218531 MIME stands for Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions.  MIME is an Internet standard defined in RFC1521.  MIME defines the format for embedding multimedia object types, such as picture files, in standard SMTP mail messages.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Moderator: }{\insrsid7218531 A moderator is a user who is responsible for managing a mail list. Moderators can define the parameters associated with the list, set the mail list's membership, and control the content of messages posted to the list.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Ping: }{\insrsid7218531 Ping is a command line utility used to verify and diagnose TCP/IP operations.  You can use Ping to verify network connectivity to another computer by entering: }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 ping hostname}{\insrsid7218531  on the command line.  Ping will attempt to resolve the hostname to a TCP/IP address by connecting to the DNS server specified in the }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 Control Panel: Network: TCP/IP}{\insrsid7218531  configuration.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Postmaster: }{\insrsid7218531 A postmaster is the e-mail administrator for an Internet site. Postmasters typically create and delete mailboxes, grant access privileges to other users, and generally run the e-mail system. E-mail administrators can typically be contacted at }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 postmaster@domain.com}{\insrsid7218531 .
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 POP: }{\insrsid7218531 POP stands for Post Office Protocol.  POP is an Internet standard defined in RFC1725.  POP defines the language that computers use when transferring incoming mail messages from a mail server to a mail client over a TCP/IP network.  }{\insrsid13722995 WorldMail}{\insrsid7218531  has implemented this standard in the POP3A Service.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 RAS: }{\insrsid7218531 Remote Access Service (RAS) provides network connectivity for an NT machine over the serial (modem) port.  The capability of this connection nearly matches the capability of a direct network connection using an Ethernet board, for example.  The main difference is that some event needs to make and break the RAS connection\'3b whereas an Ethernet connection is always available.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Registry: }{\insrsid7218531 The Registry contains all of the information related to the hardware and software configuration of your computer.  The mail server makes several entries in the Registry to record information about the mail services, the mail configuration and the mail users.  The Registry entries are created and maintained through the Installation Wizard and the }{\insrsid8736596 Management Center}{\insrsid7218531  program.  Do not make any direct changes to the registry unless instructed to do so by technical support.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 RFC: }{\insrsid7218531 Request For Comment (RFC) is the term for the standards setting process used by the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF).  Each Internet standard has a unique RFC number.  For example, the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) standard is described in RFC821.  A complete list of RFCs can be found at http://www.rfc-editor.org.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 SMTP: }{\insrsid7218531 SMTP stands for Simple Mail Transfer Protocol.  SMTP is an Internet standard defined in RFC821.  SMTP is an application level network protocol that runs on top of TCP/IP.  It defines the language that computers use when transferring mail messages between machines over a network.  The mail server has implemented this standard in the SMTPRA Service.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Sniffer: }{\insrsid7218531 A sniffer is a program that captures data exchanged on the Internet. Sniffers can be used to capture sensitive information, such as mailbox passwords transmitted by e-mail clients.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 Spam: }{\insrsid7218531 Spam is the name commonly used to describe unsolicited commercial e-mail.
\par }{\b\insrsid7218531 TCP/IP: }{\insrsid7218531 TCP/IP is the acronym given to the suite of networking protocols that define the communication language between computers.  The protocols originated in the UNIX world and have been ported to the Personal Computer, Minicomputer and Mainframe worlds.  TCP/IP stands for Transmission Control Protocol / Internet Protocol.  Each computer on a TCP/IP network has a name and address.  The full name is similar to }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 host.company.com}{\insrsid7218531  where }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 company.com}{\insrsid7218531  is the domain name.  The address is similar to }{\b\f2\insrsid7218531 123.45.67.89}{\insrsid7218531 .  HOSTS tables or DNS servers provide the name to address conversions.}{\b\insrsid7218531 
